# Auto-detected by trivy with no flag needed (default --ignorefile is
# .trivyignore) -- deliberately global rather than path-scoped, because
# trivy-action's own path-scoped `.trivyignore.yaml` + trivyignores input
# does not correctly re-apply the severity threshold before deciding the
# job's exit code when format is sarif (observed: findings well below
# CRITICAL/HIGH still failed the job). Only one ID, with a narrow,
# documented reason -- see deploy/kustomize/base/rbac.yaml for the
# rationale in context.
#
# AVD-DS-0002 (backend/Dockerfile.dev, frontend/Dockerfile.dev running as
# root) used to be ignored here on the theory that root kept the
# bind-mounted source tree and shared dependency-cache volumes writable
# without matching host UIDs. Both Dockerfiles now run as a uid-1000
# non-root user instead (chowning the mounted paths so a fresh volume
# still comes up writable), so the finding is fixed rather than accepted
# -- do not re-add it without re-litigating that decision.

# resources: ["*"] on kubemg-custom-resource-{view,edit} only ever reaches
# the enumerated, non-core API groups those ClusterRoles list (Gateway API +
# Istio) -- never the core group, where Secrets live.
AVD-KSV-0046

# KSV-0053 flags any Role granting `create` on pods/exec, because exec into a
# pod carrying a privileged service account token is a standard path to
# cluster-admin. Here it is the feature rather than an oversight:
# kubemg-shell-runner exists so that KubeMG can open a terminal in a shell pod
# it created, which is the whole browser shell.
#
# Four things bound it, and they are why this is accepted rather than fixed:
#
#   * It is a Role, not a ClusterRole, so it reaches one namespace.
#   * Its subject is the *user* kubemg:shell-runner -- not a group, not a
#     service account. Nothing in the cluster authenticates as that name; only
#     KubeMG can assert it, through the agent's impersonation grant.
#   * The pod it execs into holds no service account token at all
#     (automountServiceAccountToken: false, on an account granted nothing), so
#     the shell it opens carries none of the privilege this rule warns about.
#   * Every exec KubeMG makes names `kubemg-shell-<user id>`, built server-side
#     from the session -- no pod name reaches that path from a caller.
#
# The residual, stated plainly: RBAC has no pattern match and shell pod names
# are per-user, so the rule cannot be narrowed with resourceNames and does
# therefore cover the agent's own pod, which holds a token with cluster-wide
# impersonate. Nothing addresses that pod today, and the guard is that the name
# is derived rather than accepted. Running shell pods in a namespace of their
# own would remove the overlap entirely; the agent's namespace was chosen
# deliberately instead, and this is the trade that came with that choice.
AVD-KSV-0053
